Extension API: put the ship node table and the fleet box selection on
the wire.

REVISION 2 (15 September 2026, brief 119). Appends two OPTIONAL trailing
blocks to STATE_SNAPSHOT, after the new-game values at the very end of
ext::SerializeState():

  1. one uint8 per ship icon, the icon's owner, 0xFF when no ship stands
     behind it — doc/ext_ship_icon_owner.patch, CARRIED HERE, because
     OrionLayer reads exactly _ship_icon_count bytes after the new-game
     values as owners (core/game_state.py) and block 2 has to come after
     them. A client that only knows block 1 stops there and is unaffected.
     Unchanged from revision 1.
  2. "FSEL" (4 bytes);
     int16 the stack the fleet box shows (MOX::_fleet_box_ship_stack, -1
       while box 2 is closed or the index is out of range);
     int16 N, the number of ship nodes in use (MOX::_next_free_node,
       clamped to MAX_SHIPS);
     N times: int16 MOX::_ship_node[i].ship_idx, uint8
       MOX::_ship_node[i].selected != 0 — the flag
       HACCESS::Get_Fleet_Box_Selected_Ship_Ids_ builds a move order from;
     int16 L, the length of the fleet box stack's chain (0 while closed);
     L times: int16 node, from MOX::_ship_stack_start[stack] along
       next_node — the order FLEETPOP builds the box's ship cells in
       (fleetpop.cpp:643-676; the first visible cell is
       _fltpop_scroll_bar.first_visible_row * 3, which is NOT sent).

WHAT CHANGED AGAINST REVISION 1 (applied and built 15 September 2026,
brief 118, then taken back out of the tree):
    * per node the SHIP INDEX is sent next to the selected flag (3 bytes
      per node instead of 1);
    * the fleet box stack's chain is sent, in display order;
    * the stack is -1 also when the index is out of range, not only
      while the box is closed.
    Block 1, the "FSEL" marker and the position of the block are
    unchanged. A revision-1 engine against a revision-2 client ends the
    snapshot 2N + 2 bytes short of what the client needs, and the client
    reads no block at all.

WHY REVISION 2 (measured live, 15 September 2026, scratch save SAVE5,
one client, brief 118): revision 1 said node i is the i-th ship with
status < 3, which a client can rebuild. It is not.
SHIPSTAK::Find_Ship_Stacks_ numbers the nodes that way, and then
SHIPSTAK::Sort_Ships_In_Stack_ (shipstak.cpp:261-278) qsorts each stack's
ships by type and WRITES ship_idx back along the chain: the node places
stay, the ships in them move. On the Yoth stack (ships 13, 14, 15)
OrionLayer sent MSG_SELECT_SHIP for ship 13, which it took for node 9;
the engine flipped node 11, and the original's THIRD cell turned black,
not the first. qsort is not stable, so a client cannot reproduce the sort
either (two identical warships compare equal). The table has to come off
the wire.

Open fix 20 in doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md; briefs 116, 118 and 119.

